  NOT SUPPRESSION -- but 2 of 4 gains differ past 3 SE, and 2 of those
  go the OTHER WAY, with teaching raising the amplitude. Read the signs in
  the table before calling this a null: a residual that is a few per cent
  of its own amplitude and CHANGES SIGN across the sweep is the shape of a
  tiny systematic difference measured at high precision, not of a
  mechanism, and the composition problem is still the lesson's side. A
  residual that holds one sign and grows with gain would be a mechanism
  and is not what this is.

  THEY DO NOT COMPOSE, AND THE CROSSING IS NOW MEASURED RATHER THAN
  INTERPOLATED. The best lesson over this sweep is +0.2062 at 1.40 against a
  bar of 0.1099. Three generations have now swept both axes of the
  oscillating window -- gain 0.15-0.60 at drive 1.40, drive 0.90-1.40 at
  gain 0.45, and the gain interval 0.45-0.54 that the first two straddled.
  Nothing clears both bars anywhere.

  THE CLOSEST APPROACH IS BOUNDED FROM BOTH SIDES. Where the gesture
  first reaches sep 10, the lesson is 3.7 SE BELOW its own bar; where the
  lesson clears, the gesture is at 8.0 and falling. So this is not a
  knife-edge that more resolution could find -- the two requirements are
  satisfied on disjoint stretches with a resolved gap between them.

  That makes it a statement about the architecture: a scalar reward
  writing into a rate-weighted centroid that a mutual-inhibition attractor
  is already holding at one of its two ends.

  WHAT WOULD STILL OVERTURN IT: a cell in the CORNERS of the window --
  every sweep so far has moved one axis with the other fixed, so low drive
  crossed with high gain is untested, and `sep` is not monotone in gain
  (8.0 at 0.45, 6.7 at 0.48, 10.0 at 0.51), which is exactly the shape that
  makes a one-axis sweep a poor guide to a two-axis surface.
